that's inversely proportional to the square of the distance $d$ from the source to the object detected by the sensor. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the intensity of the light source $I?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **intensity** $I$ of a light source with a motion sensor **increases** at a rate that's **inversely proportional** to the **square of the distance** $d$ from the source to the object detected by the sensor.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *intensity* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}I}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}I}{\\text{d}t}$ is *inversely proportional to* the *square of the distance.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}I}{\\text{d}t}∝\\frac{1}{{d}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the intensity *increases* with time, our derivative should be *positive*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}I}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{{d}^{2}}.\n$$"}]}
